{
  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_002 (rna_only, multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool) achieved 0.591 acc / 0.573 F1, the best result so far, substantially outperforming the singleprofile baseline (config_001, 0.485 acc). However, the singleprofile→multiprofile jump also introduced an aggregation step, so we cannot yet tell whether the gain comes from the multiprofile representation itself or from the aggregation mechanism. Question: config_003 (multiprofile, pca_n2000, transformer) directly compares transformer aggregation against avgpool while holding all other factors constant, isolating the effect of the aggregation method. Why this is the best next experiment: it is a clean one-variable comparison against our current best, requires no new encoders or modalities, and tells us whether a more expressive aggregation (transformer) is worth the added complexity before we invest budget in learned encoders (scvi, scgpt) or multimodal fusion. If transformer significantly beats avgpool, it signals that richer aggregation is a key design lever; if not, we can focus budget on encoder choice and multimodal combinations with confidence that simple averaging suffices."
}

